;; The ninth <entity>_categories table.
;;
;; `messages` was the one entity with no categories, which was a real design
;; position and not an oversight: the Inbox is a triage queue, and the thing you
;; categorise was the task or resource a message got converted into. The Inbox
;; growing the shared Category sidebar is what made that position untenable to
;; look at — the box was right there, showing a selection, and the card under it
;; had no way to take one.
;;
;; Shaped exactly like `issue_categories`, cascade included — and the cascade is
;; declared for the shape rather than for the effect. **SQLite enforces foreign
;; keys only under `PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON`, which this app never sets**, so
;; every `ON DELETE CASCADE` in this schema is decoration and the eight tables
;; this one copies all leave rows behind today. Written the same way anyway,
;; because a table that disagreed with its siblings would read as a decision, and
;; because the day the pragma goes on this one should already be right.
;;
;; What actually removes these rows is named code: `db.message/drop-category-links!`
;; and `move-category-links!`, called from the delete and from both conversions.
;; That is not belt-and-braces here the way it would be elsewhere — messages are
;; the one entity deleted rather than archived, and the Inbox is a queue whose
;; purpose is to empty, so orphans would arrive continuously rather than rarely.
;;
;; `category_type` is the same denormalised mirror of `categories.category_type`
;; that migration 073 describes, and this table is now one of the ones the
;; group-move has to keep in step — see `db.category/join-tables`, which is the
;; registry that says so.
{:up ["CREATE TABLE message_categories (
         message_id INTEGER NOT NULL,
         category_type TEXT NOT NULL,
         category_id INTEGER NOT NULL,
         PRIMARY KEY (message_id, category_type, category_id),
         FOREIGN KEY (message_id) REFERENCES messages(id) ON DELETE CASCADE
       )"]
 :down ["DROP TABLE message_categories"]}
